A||||19||fiero56||still searching for clutch suggestions||||5||
Z||000000||fiero56||10-22-2001||11:27 PM||||I'm working on a 2.8 biuld(getrag tranny), and haven't decided on a good clutch for the setup.<P>My 2 major criteria are:<BR>1. clutch must engage smoothly<BR>2. Clutch must clamp tight enough to allow for some spirited driving.<P>I don't care about cost, clutch life, or materials.<P>My current RAM clutch doesn't meet either of my criteria, and will never use one again. <P>I've read every thread I could find in the archives, some suggest Borg-Warner, others C/F, others etc. <P>I'm looking for recommendations with reasons on why a particular clutch would be a good choice for me. This is my daily driver, but clutch life doesn't really matter, I just want something smooth and something that bites tight enough I can chirp the tires when I feel like it.<P>Relatively light pedal feel is a plus too.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 10-22-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000001||Joe Torma||10-23-2001||12:45 AM||||I like Centerforces.  Smooth and not tough to disengage.||||reg||7
Z||000002||fierobear||10-23-2001||01:27 AM||||Fiero Shop/Superior Friction. I've driven several Fieros with them, including 2.8's and a 3.4. They do all you're asking.||||reg||1
Z||000003||BOILERMAKER||10-23-2001||08:26 AM||||I personally like the LUK clutch that I have in my Getrag.  Engagement is a touch tougher mainly due to the pressure plate, but I like it.  You can ask Scott about it because he has driven it.  You are welcome to drive it anytime I have it close to you.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fierobear||10-23-2001||03:38 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BOILERMAKER:<BR><B>I personally like the LUK clutch that I have in my Getrag.  Engagement is a touch tougher mainly due to the pressure plate, but I like it.  You can ask Scott about it because he has driven it.  You are welcome to drive it anytime I have it close to you.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Be careful with LUK clutches. The funny thing is, they are the 'official' clutch for the car, but LUK does NOT warrantee the clutch in the Fiero. Read the disclaimer that comes with the clutch.||||reg||1
Z||000005||thomas_l||10-23-2001||03:57 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fierobear:<BR><B> Be careful with LUK clutches. The funny thing is, they are the 'official' clutch for the car, but LUK does NOT warrantee the clutch in the Fiero. Read the disclaimer that comes with the clutch.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I put a Luk in my 87 GT and autocross it more than street driving. Works for me. If it explodes I'll tell them it was in a 86 Cavalier. I actually had to use that Cavalier model parts lookup to get the right T/O bearing since the mail-order tranny I bought turned out to be a 4 speed. I have installed dozens of Luks and had only one come back due to a dead pressure plate - and that was in a VW Golf.||||reg||1
Z||000006||DXR_DAD||10-23-2001||04:33 PM||||What's up Mr.Bear??<P><BR>DXR_DAD||||reg||10
Z||000007||fierobear||10-23-2001||04:49 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by thomas_l:<BR><B>turned out to be a 4 speed. I have installed dozens of Luks and had only one come back due to a dead pressure plate - and that was in a VW Golf.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The only good part of an LUK is the pressure plate, as far as clamp load goes. The problem is, the ends of the fingers tend to wear out too fast.||||reg||1
Z||000008||fierobear||10-23-2001||04:50 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by DXR_DAD:<BR><B>What's up Mr.Bear??<P><BR>DXR_DAD</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Hey, Bill. Whassuuuuup???||||reg||1
Z||000009||BOILERMAKER||10-23-2001||04:53 PM||||My opinion still remains the same.  They work great!||||reg||1
Z||000010||Jimmy||10-23-2001||08:03 PM||||I think the CF DF clutch in my V8 setup would be great for a built up V6.  Funny thing is if you look at the DF disc from CF its stamped with a "LUK" symbol. They must make clutch parts for a lot of manufactures.<P>Jim<BR>87 GT T-TOP V8<BR>87 GT<BR>MSCF||||reg||1
Z||000011||fierobear||10-23-2001||08:14 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by BOILERMAKER:<BR><B>My opinion still remains the same.  They work great!</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>How many *miles* do you have on your LUK clutch?||||reg||1
Z||000012||chester||10-24-2001||07:15 PM||||When I bought my car the clutch was bad. I had a LUK clutch installed. I didn't like it.  After some spirited driving you would feel the clutch start to slip.  The more it got abused the worse it got.  I have since gone to a Centerforce dual friction clutch.  The pedal is light and the engagement is positive.  Does not fade. (or hasn't on me yet  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> ).<P>Rob D.<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigline.jpg">   <BR>Modified 2.8<BR>2.5" Drop<BR>11" Brakes<BR>17" Revolutions<BR>RCC Coilover Suspension||||reg||1
Z||000013||fiero56||10-24-2001||09:44 PM||||Center Force is sounding like a good possibility. Has anyone ever experienced the c/f clutch being extremely jerky when cold, or slipping when hot? My RAM does both. <P>Any recommendations on who to get the c/f clutch through?||||reg||1
Z||000014||BOILERMAKER||10-25-2001||09:23 AM||||I have only around 10K miles on this second LUK clutch and very many of them are extremely spirited miles.  I can push it as much as I want with my 3.2L and it never slips.  The only time it came close was in a huge traffic jam for over an hour when I was riding it often.  No jerkiness at all.<P>This is the second LUK clutch.  The first one was put in who knows how long ago (car has around 146K miles on it and was previously owned by a pilot) and was still semi-good, but with the new engine, I decided to go with a new clutch as well.  For the 2.8/3.2, I will not use anything else.  Of course, to each their own.<P>Fierobear, your opinion on the LUK clutch is just that, an opinion.  I respect it, but don't agree with it.||||reg||1
Z||000015||fiero87||10-25-2001||11:40 AM||||Hey everyone,<P>I am building a 3.4 with ported heads, cam, etc...  Also planning on some nitrous down the line.  Any suggestions for my setup?  I was looking at the fieroshop's with the 2400lb pressure plate.  Any opinions?  Thanks<BR>Tim||||reg||1
Z||000016||Joe Torma||10-25-2001||12:06 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by fiero56:<BR><B>Center Force is sounding like a good possibility. Has anyone ever experienced the c/f clutch being extremely jerky when cold, or slipping when hot? My RAM does both. <P>Any recommendations on who to get the c/f clutch through?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>If you get any new clutch, make sure the entire hydraulic system is in perfect order.  I was having trouble with my centerforce and it ended up warping and hot-spotting my flywheel bad.  But I see now that it was never really disengaging properly because the master cyl was shot(and slave too)<P>MY centerforce has never been jerky or anything....aside from the above problem.||||reg||6
Z||000017||fiero56||10-25-2001||01:55 PM||||I shouldn't have any problems. I plan to replace master, slave, and flywheel just like I did 20K miles ago when I put the RAM in. I don't reuse anything clutch related. I will either end up pitching the pieces in the trash, or offer them up for sale here. Haven't decided if its worth my time to mess with going to the post office to ship them yet.||||reg||1
Z||000018||fiero56||10-25-2001||02:09 PM||||I see I have two choices with CenterForce.<P>C/F I -#MS361914<BR>C/F DF - #201914<P>CENTERFORCE® I<P>(the original) - offers 30% more holding capacity than a stock<BR>'OEM' clutch while maintaining a stock pedal feel. This<BR>system is recommended as the finest quality 'upgrade' from<BR>the original equipment clutch.<P>CENTERFORCE DUAL FRICTION®<P>Generates up to a 90% increase in holding capacity over<BR>stock systems. This clutch/disc set is designed for use with<BR>engines producing 'high horsepower and torque' output, and<BR>any radically altered high performance street vehicles. This<BR>system is also ideally suited for use with four wheel drive<BR>vehicles. All Centerforce® clutch systems provide smooth<BR>engagement and light pedal pressure without compromising<BR>high performance.<P>Any thoughts on these?||||reg||1
Z||000019||GT GT 87||10-25-2001||04:18 PM||||I am building a 3.2 , and am asking----Has anyone used these 2 diferent C/F clutches and how are they ??????<P>------------------<BR>||||reg||1
